        "id": "ch10_2_2-1",
        "text": "Risk calculation\nRisk assessment , also known as risk calculation or risk analysis , refers to the action of finding and\ncalculating solutions to possible threats and vulnerabilities. The following are some basic terms you\nshould know when you talk about risk impact:\n\u2022I The annual loss expectancy ( ALE ) defines the loss that\u2019s expected in 1 year.\n\u2022I The single loss expectancy ( SLE ) represents how much loss is expected at any given time.\n\u2022 The annual rate of occurrence ( ARO ) is the likeliness of a risky event occurring within 1 year.\n\u2022 The risk calculation formula is SLE x ARO = ALE . There is a monetary value that each element\nof the formula will provide, so the final result is also expressed as a monetary value. This is a\nformula that is useful to know.\n\u2022 The mean time between failures ( MTBF ) is used to measure the time between anticipated\nand reparable failures.\n\u2022 The mean time to failure ( MTTF ) is the average time the system can operate before experiencing\nan irreparable failure.\n\u2022I The mean time to restore ( MTTR ) measures the time needed to repair an affected system.\n\u2022 The recovery time objective ( RTO ) represents the maximum time that\u2019s allocated for downtime.\n\u2022I The recovery point objective ( RPO ) defines the time when a system needs to be restored.\nKnowing those terms will help you understand risk assessments so that you can perform a well-\ndocumented assessment if or when needed. Risk assessment is based on two major types of actions\n(or better said, strategies):\n\u2022 Proactive actions :\nI Risk avoidance : Based on risk identification and finding a quick solution to avoid its occurrence\nI Risk mitigation : Based on actions taken to reduce the occurrence of a possible risk\nI Risk transference : Transferring the risk\u2019s possible outcome with an external entity\nI Risk deterrence : Based on specific systems and policies that should discourage any attacker\nfrom exploiting the system\n\u2022 Non-active actions :\nI Risk acceptance : Accepting the risk if the other proactive actions could exceed the cost of\nthe harm that\u2019s done by the risk\nPlanning for disaster recovery\n387\nThe strategies described here can be applied to the risk associated with generic, on-premises computing,",
